   "text": {
    "L1": "On a sunny day, the Sun heats both the land and the sea. Land heats up faster than water.",
    "L2": "Air over the hot land warms, expands, becomes lighter, and rises.",
    "L3": "Cooler, heavier air over the sea sinks and moves in to take its place.",
    "L4": "That moving air is convection: warm air rises while cool air sinks, making a circulating current.",
    "L5": "So the daytime sea breeze blows from the sea towards the land.",
    "L6": "At night, land cools faster than the sea. Air over the warmer sea rises, and cooler air moves from land towards the sea.",
    "L7": "Heat can also travel sideways through a spoon by conduction or from a heater by radiation. Heat does not only move upward.",
    "L8": "Now check the direction of the daytime breeze."
   },
